# Target C — all-phase harmonic concentration audit

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module combines canonical Section 75 with the harmonic attachment cells from
Section 62. Its point is to remove one remaining diffuse-core possibility.

A quadratic positive KKT contact core cannot distribute its quadratic population over
many small harmonic components. In every post-ZF phase, positive quadratic core
density forces a bounded family of graph-diameter-`Theta(D)` unloaded components to
carry a positive fraction of the core, and one of those components itself contains
`Theta(D^2)` unloaded vertices. The same component has a loaded attachment polygon of
macroscopic Euclidean diameter.

The theorem is an all-phase rebuild of the useful finite-packing mechanism in the old
unreviewed report
`docs/reports/target_c_quadratic_population_harmonic_component_2026-09-09.md`.
That report used a near-circle diameter budget. Here canonical Section 75 supplies the
global replacement

\[
\sum_r\delta_r\le 2+2\pi D,
\qquad
c_I=O(D^{2/3}),
\]

so no near-circle, near-minimal-boundary, `s_Z=0`, or Woodall-branch hypothesis
remains.

---

# 77. Positive quadratic core density forces a quadratic harmonic component

Use the canonical post-ZF notation from Sections 62 and 75:

- `B` is the loaded set and `q=|B|`;
- `I_1,...,I_{c_I}` are the connected components of the unloaded nonisolated
  positive-contact graph after deleting `B`;
- `n_r=|I_r|`;
- `A_r subset B` is the distinct loaded attachment set of `I_r`;
- `P_r=conv(A_r)`;
- `delta_r` is the graph diameter of `C[I_r]`, with `delta_r=0` for a singleton;
- `m` is the population of the nonisolated positive-contact core.

The sets `B,I_1,...,I_{c_I}` partition that core, so

\[
\boxed{m=q+\sum_r n_r.}
\tag{77.1}
\]

Canonical harmonic trapping and the contact-path estimate give

\[
I_r\subseteq P_r,
\qquad
\operatorname{diam}(P_r)\le\delta_r+2.
\tag{77.2}
\]

Canonical Section 75 gives

\[
\boxed{c_I=O(D^{2/3})}
\tag{77.3}
\]

and, from its finite diameter ledger,

\[
\boxed{
S_D:=\sum_r\delta_r\le 2+2\pi D.
}
\tag{77.4}
\]

Finally `q<=pi D`, so the loaded population is boundary-order.

Put

\[
\Delta:={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{77.5}
\]

## 77.1 Finite capacity of one harmonic component

### Lemma 77.1 — component Oler capacity

Every unloaded harmonic component satisfies

\[
\boxed{
 n_r
\le
1+\Delta(\delta_r+2)^2
+{\pi\over2}(\delta_r+2).
}
\tag{77.6}
\]

### Proof

The `n_r` points of `I_r` are unit-separated and lie in the convex set `P_r`.
Canonical Oler Lemma 21.1 gives

\[
n_r
\le {2\over\sqrt3}\operatorname{area}(P_r)
+{1\over2}\operatorname{per}(P_r)+1.
\tag{77.7}
\]

Let `d_r=diam(P_r)`. The planar isodiametric inequality and Cauchy's
perimeter-diameter inequality give

\[
\operatorname{area}(P_r)\le {\pi d_r^2\over4},
\qquad
\operatorname{per}(P_r)\le\pi d_r.
\tag{77.8}
\]

Since `d_r<=delta_r+2`, substitution into (77.7) gives (77.6). `square`

In particular there are absolute constants `C_1,C_0` such that

\[
n_r\le\Delta\delta_r^2+C_1\delta_r+C_0.
\tag{77.9}
\]

Only the leading coefficient `Delta` matters below.

## 77.2 Small graph-diameter components carry little quadratic population

Fix one constant `eta>0` and split

\[
\mathcal S_\eta:=\{r:\delta_r<\eta D\},
\qquad
\mathcal L_\eta:=\{r:\delta_r\ge\eta D\}.
\tag{77.10}
\]

For the small components,

\[
\sum_{r\in\mathcal S_\eta}\delta_r^2
\le
\eta D\sum_r\delta_r.
\]

Using (77.3), (77.4), and (77.9),

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{r\in\mathcal S_\eta}n_r
&\le
\Delta\eta D S_D+C_1S_D+C_0c_I\\
&\le
2\pi\Delta\eta D^2+o(D^2).
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\limsup_{D\to\infty}
{1\over D^2}
\sum_{r\in\mathcal S_\eta}n_r
\le2\pi\Delta\eta.
}
\tag{77.11}
\]

The large components are few. Equation (77.4) gives

\[
|\mathcal L_\eta|\,\eta D
\le S_D,
\]

hence

\[
\boxed{
\limsup_{D\to\infty}|\mathcal L_\eta|
\le {2\pi\over\eta}.
}
\tag{77.12}
\]

More precisely, for every sufficiently large `D`,

\[
|\mathcal L_\eta|
\le {2\pi\over\eta}+{2\over\eta D}.
\tag{77.13}
\]

So for every fixed macroscopic diameter threshold, only boundedly many harmonic
components can cross it.

## 77.3 Quantitative concentration theorem

### Theorem 77.2 — quadratic core concentration

Consider any survivor subsequence for which

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {m\over D^2}\ge r>0.
}
\tag{77.14}
\]

Set

\[
\boxed{
\eta_*:={r\over4\pi\Delta}.
}
\tag{77.15}
\]

Then:

1. components with `delta_r<eta_*D` carry at most half of the guaranteed quadratic
   core density:
   \[
   \boxed{
   \limsup {1\over D^2}
   \sum_{\delta_r<\eta_*D}n_r
   \le {r\over2};
   }
   \tag{77.16}
   \]
2. the number of components with `delta_r>=eta_*D` is bounded by
   \[
   \boxed{
   \limsup
   \#\{r:\delta_r\ge\eta_*D\}
   \le {8\pi^2\Delta\over r};
   }
   \tag{77.17}
   \]
3. one harmonic component satisfies
   \[
   \boxed{
   \liminf
   \max_r {n_r\over D^2}
   \ge {r^2\over16\pi^2\Delta};
   }
   \tag{77.18}
   \]
4. choosing a component realizing the maximum in (77.18), its loaded attachment
   polygon is macroscopically wide:
   \[
   \boxed{
   \liminf
   {\operatorname{diam}(P_r)\over D}
   \ge {r\over4\pi\Delta}=\eta_*.
   }
   \tag{77.19}
   \]

Hence a positive-density positive-contact core contains an actual connected unloaded
positive-contact component with `Theta(D^2)` vertices inside a loaded attachment cell
of diameter `Theta(D)`.

### Proof

Because `q<=pi D`, equations (77.1) and (77.14) give

\[
\liminf {1\over D^2}\sum_rn_r\ge r.
\tag{77.20}
\]

Insert `eta=eta_*` into (77.11). Since

\[
2\pi\Delta\eta_*={r\over2},
\]

we obtain (77.16). Therefore the components in `mathcal L_{eta_*}` carry at least

\[
\left({r\over2}-o(1)\right)D^2
\tag{77.21}
\]

unloaded vertices.

Equation (77.12) gives

\[
\limsup|\mathcal L_{\eta_*}|
\le {2\pi\over\eta_*}
={8\pi^2\Delta\over r},
\]

which is (77.17). Pigeonholing (77.21) over this bounded family gives

\[
\liminf\max_r{n_r\over D^2}
\ge
{{r/2}\over{8\pi^2\Delta/r}}
={r^2\over16\pi^2\Delta}.
\]

This proves (77.18).

It remains to prove the attachment-span statement for a maximizing component. Write

\[
d_r=\operatorname{diam}(P_r).
\]

Applying Oler directly as in (77.7)-(77.8), without replacing `d_r` by
`delta_r+2`, gives

\[
 n_r
\le
1+\Delta d_r^2+{\pi\over2}d_r.
\tag{77.22}
\]

If along a further subsequence `d_r/D -> s`, then (77.18) and (77.22) imply

\[
{r^2\over16\pi^2\Delta}
\le \Delta s^2.
\]

Thus

\[
s\ge {r\over4\pi\Delta}=\eta_*.
\]

Since every further convergent subsequence has this lower bound, (77.19) follows.
`square`

## 77.4 Optimized form and comparison with the old near-circle report

The constants above are the exact optimizer of the all-phase Section-75 diameter
budget. For arbitrary fixed `eta`, (77.11)-(77.12) give

\[
\liminf\max_r{n_r\over D^2}
\ge
{\eta\over2\pi}
\left(r-2\pi\Delta\eta\right)
\tag{77.23}
\]

whenever the bracket is positive. The right side is maximized at

\[
\eta={r\over4\pi\Delta},
\]

and its maximum is exactly the coefficient in (77.18).

The old unreviewed near-circle report had the stronger local premise

\[
\sum_r\delta_r\le(\pi+o(1))D,
\]

which yielded `r^2/(4pi^2Delta)`. That coefficient must not be imported at the present
all-phase scope. Canonical Section 75 gives `2pi` rather than `pi` as the universal
leading diameter-budget coefficient, so the correct all-phase constant is

\[
{r^2\over16\pi^2\Delta}.
\]

This module therefore supersedes the old report only for the all-phase statement; it
does not claim the old near-circle constant was algebraically wrong under its own
stronger premise.

## 77.5 Interaction with the quadratic rhombus branch

Canonical Section 69 gives

\[
m=R_4+O(D).
\tag{77.24}
\]

Hence the quadratic positive-core branch is exactly the branch in which (77.14) holds
for some fixed `r>0`. Theorem 77.2 shows that this branch has two simultaneous forms
of concentration:

1. Sections 69 and 73 concentrate `Theta(D^2)` rhombi into a coherent physical train
   grain and then extract a linear complete phase-pure train grid;
2. Section 77 concentrates `Theta(D^2)` positive-contact vertices into one harmonic
   attachment component whose loaded attachment polygon has macroscopic span.

These two concentrated objects are not yet proved to be the same subcomplex. That is
the next coupling problem. A valid closure must either show that a positive fraction
of the dominant coherent grain lies in one of the boundedly many macroscopic harmonic
components, or derive a contradiction if the coherent grain crosses many attachment
cells/interfaces.

The point is that the quadratic branch is no longer allowed to hide behind a diffuse
family of `D^{2/3}` tiny harmonic components.

## 77.6 What this theorem does not control

The theorem does **not** show that

- the largest-population harmonic component is the same component as the dominant
  Section-69 coherent rhombic grain;
- one attachment set has linearly many loaded vertices;
- contact-isolated source points are few;
- the subquadratic positive-core branch is impossible;
- a KKT weight has a positive lower bound;
- the attachment polygon is disk-like or a Reuleaux cap.

Contact-isolated source points remain in the full source and are outside every count
`n_r` in this theorem.

## 77.7 Dependency and regression audit

- **Section 62 harmonic trapping:** `I_r subset conv(A_r)` and
  `diam(P_r)<=delta_r+2`.
- **Canonical Oler Lemma 21.1:** finite unit-separated capacity in a convex container.
- **Section 75:** `c_I=O(D^(2/3))` and
  `sum delta_r<=2+2pi D` at full post-ZF scope.
- **Loaded hull:** `q<=pi D`, so loaded vertices do not carry quadratic population.
- **Section 69:** used only for the interpretation (77.24), not for Theorem 77.2.
- **Near-circle reports:** not used as premises.
- **Contact isolates:** retained globally and not counted by the nonisolated core.

## 77.8 Compact handoff

```text
LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.

Canonical Section 75:
    c_I = O(D^(2/3)),
    S_D=sum delta_r <= 2+2pi D.

One harmonic component:
    I_r subset P_r=conv(A_r),
    diam(P_r)<=delta_r+2,
    Oler => n_r <= Delta delta_r^2 + O(delta_r+1),
    Delta=pi/(2sqrt3).

For fixed eta:
    components delta_r<eta D carry at most
        (2pi Delta eta + o(1))D^2,
    number of components delta_r>=eta D is at most
        2pi/eta + o(1).

If liminf m/D^2 >= r>0, choose eta*=r/(4pi Delta):
    small components carry <=(r/2+o(1))D^2,
    at most 8pi^2 Delta/r macroscopic components remain,
    one has
        n_r >= (r^2/(16pi^2 Delta)-o(1))D^2,
    and its attachment polygon satisfies
        diam(P_r) >= (r/(4pi Delta)-o(1))D.

New live coupling target:
    force overlap between this quadratic harmonic component
    and the canonical quadratic coherent rhombic grain,
    or charge separation between them to the O(D) interface budget.
```
